  eps=2.000000 (archive-relevant, eps<=sqrt(pi/2): False): max h_eps = 1.9979920081 at z=500.0000  (sqrt(pi/2)=1.2533141373)
  eps=5.000000 (archive-relevant, eps<=sqrt(pi/2): False): max h_eps = 4.9979800082 at z=500.0000  (sqrt(pi/2)=1.2533141373)
  eps=10.000000 (archive-relevant, eps<=sqrt(pi/2): False): max h_eps = 9.9979600085 at z=500.0000  (sqrt(pi/2)=1.2533141373)

Reading: for eps <= sqrt(pi/2) (all archive-relevant eps=1/sqrt(c), c>=1),
the max is at z=0 and equals sqrt(pi/2), confirming the two-case proof's
conclusion on the relevant domain. For eps > sqrt(pi/2) (eps=1.5,2,5,10,
OUTSIDE this lineage's regime), the max can exceed sqrt(pi/2) -- confirming
the proof's 'eps<=sqrt(pi/2)' hypothesis is NOT vacuous: it is genuinely
load-bearing for eps>sqrt(pi/2), where Case 2's final step 'eps<=sqrt(pi/2)'
would fail and the bound sqrt(pi/2) would NOT hold globally.

